WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.582
Part Five explained why the Landau argument takes concentrated
weighted readings of the signal.

00:00:06.105 --> 00:00:10.080
The weights contain powers, factorials, and exponential damping.

00:00:10.603 --> 00:00:16.324
Those expressions look continuous, but we need finite computations
with justified error bounds.

00:00:16.847 --> 00:00:21.489
This episode builds the scalar kernel weights from exact rational
arithmetic.

00:00:22.012 --> 00:00:30.073
We will first enclose an exponential, then reverse that enclosure to
obtain a decaying exponential, and finally assemble a kernel weight.

00:00:30.595 --> 00:00:35.832
After that, we will distinguish evaluating a finite sum from
approximating an integral.

00:00:36.355 --> 00:00:39.482
The error from each job has to be counted separately.

00:00:40.005 --> 00:00:45.714
The complex phases will get their own next episode, so we can build
these ingredients without rushing.

00:00:46.236 --> 00:00:51.630
This is Part Six of Landau Windows ACL Two: explicit kernels and
error budgets.

00:00:53.767 --> 00:00:57.064
The factorial in the kernel is a whole-number construction.

00:00:57.586 --> 00:01:02.495
For a positive integer, multiply all the positive integers up to
that number.

00:01:03.018 --> 00:01:13.369
One factorial is one… two factorial is two… three factorial is six…
and four factorial is twenty-four.

00:01:13.892 --> 00:01:16.534
Zero factorial is defined to be one.

00:01:17.057 --> 00:01:21.457
That convention makes the zeroth term of the exponential series
equal to one.

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:26.731
Factorials grow quickly because each new step multiplies by the next
integer.

00:01:27.254 --> 00:01:34.102
Dividing by them will eventually make successive terms smaller, even
when the numerator contains repeated multiplication.

00:01:34.625 --> 00:01:39.898
We do not need a machine's floating-point exponential operation to
begin this construction.

00:01:42.033 --> 00:01:45.452
The exponential of x has a power-series description.

00:01:45.974 --> 00:01:51.089
Its term at natural index k is x to the power k divided by k
factorial.

00:01:51.612 --> 00:01:54.121
We will use nonnegative rational x.

00:01:54.644 --> 00:01:56.789
The term at index zero is one.

00:01:57.312 --> 00:02:02.609
To get the next term, multiply the current term by x, and divide by
the new index.

00:02:03.132 --> 00:02:13.559
At x equal to one, the terms begin one… one… one half… one sixth…
and one twenty-fourth.

00:02:14.082 --> 00:02:17.039
Every one of these is an exact rational number.

00:02:17.562 --> 00:02:20.592
The individual terms are not yet the exponential.

00:02:21.115 --> 00:02:24.048
We will add them, and then bound everything left out.

00:02:26.167 --> 00:02:29.148
Here is the project's ACL2 term function.

00:02:29.671 --> 00:02:33.077
Its inputs are the rational argument and the natural index.

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:36.485
At index zero, the function returns one.

00:02:37.008 --> 00:02:43.153
Otherwise it asks for the preceding term, multiplies by the
argument, and divides by the current index.

00:02:43.676 --> 00:02:47.748
The recursive index decreases, so this is a finite computation.

00:02:48.271 --> 00:02:56.235
This recurrence produces the same quotient as the power divided by
the factorial, without computing those two large expressions
separately.

00:02:56.758 --> 00:03:00.345
For argument one and index three, it returns one sixth.

00:03:00.868 --> 00:03:03.111
That value has no rounding error.

00:03:03.633 --> 00:03:10.712
The proof task is to control omitted terms, not to guess how many
decimal places this rational number contains.

00:03:12.833 --> 00:03:14.373
Now add the terms.

00:03:14.895 --> 00:03:23.877
At argument one, the successive partial sums are one… two… five
halves… and eight thirds.

00:03:24.399 --> 00:03:29.381
The sum through index three contains four terms, because we started
at index zero.

00:03:29.904 --> 00:03:36.789
The ACL2 sum function returns one in the base case, and otherwise
adds the current term to the preceding sum.

00:03:37.311 --> 00:03:40.439
The stopping index is an ordinary natural number.

00:03:40.961 --> 00:03:44.877
Asking for a larger index gives a different finite computation.

00:03:45.399 --> 00:03:48.527
These partial sums approach the exponential of one.

00:03:49.049 --> 00:03:53.037
A finite partial sum by itself is not the final real value.

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:56.505
We need an upper bound as well as this lower sum.

00:03:58.633 --> 00:04:01.094
Take the partial sum through index three.

00:04:01.617 --> 00:04:05.192
The first omitted term at argument one is one twenty-fourth.

00:04:05.715 --> 00:04:09.412
From there, every next term is at most half its predecessor.

00:04:09.935 --> 00:04:16.577
The actual ratios in this example are smaller; using one half gives
a convenient conservative bound.

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:23.912
The omitted tail is therefore at most one twenty-fourth, plus one
forty-eighth, plus one ninety-sixth, and so on.

00:04:24.435 --> 00:04:28.108
A halving geometric series adds up to twice its first term.

00:04:28.630 --> 00:04:31.370
The whole omitted tail is at most one twelfth.

00:04:31.892 --> 00:04:42.208
For a general nonnegative argument x and stopping index n, the
condition twice x is less than or equal to n plus two ensures these
later ratios are at most one half.

00:04:42.730 --> 00:04:45.518
The ratio condition is part of the guarantee.

00:04:46.041 --> 00:04:51.144
We cannot take an arbitrary early stopping point and assume its tail
already halves.

00:04:53.267 --> 00:04:56.697
The project's band function packages those two bounds.

00:04:57.220 --> 00:05:00.008
Its lower endpoint is the finite partial sum.

00:05:00.530 --> 00:05:03.791
Its upper endpoint adds twice the first omitted term.

00:05:04.314 --> 00:05:08.483
For argument one and index three, the lower endpoint is eight
thirds.

00:05:09.006 --> 00:05:13.842
Add the tail allowance of one twelfth, and the upper endpoint is
eleven quarters.

00:05:14.365 --> 00:05:16.729
The full interval width is one twelfth.

00:05:17.252 --> 00:05:24.294
The ACL2 theorem controls every later finite partial sum, not just a
few sums that happened to be tested.

00:05:24.817 --> 00:05:30.429
This is how one finite band can make a statement about arbitrarily
long finite computations.

00:05:30.952 --> 00:05:34.794
The real exponential interpretation will use that same band.

00:05:36.933 --> 00:05:40.448
We also need a way to select a safe index from the input.

00:05:40.971 --> 00:05:43.844
The construction starts beyond twice the argument.

00:05:44.367 --> 00:05:50.985
It uses a rational rounding-up operation, returning the least whole
number at least the given nonnegative rational.

00:05:51.508 --> 00:05:56.502
Here ceiling means rounding upward, rather than the upper curve from
the Landau theorem.

00:05:57.024 --> 00:06:02.006
The bounded loop subtracts one at each step and counts how many
subtractions are needed.

00:06:02.529 --> 00:06:07.741
A computed fuel supply keeps the loop finite and is proved large
enough for its input.

00:06:08.264 --> 00:06:16.122
The wrapper supplies that fuel… and the exponential's starting index
adds one more to the rounded value of twice x.

00:06:16.645 --> 00:06:19.723
For argument one, the starting index is three.

00:06:20.246 --> 00:06:24.186
This is safely inside the region where the tail argument applies.

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:29.427
Suppose we want a full width of at most one hundredth.

00:06:29.950 --> 00:06:33.695
At argument one, the initial width allowance is one twelfth.

00:06:34.218 --> 00:06:44.436
Halve that allowance repeatedly: one twenty-fourth… one forty-
eighth… one ninety-sixth… and one one-hundred-ninety-second.

00:06:44.958 --> 00:06:48.086
The fourth halving is enough to fall below one hundredth.

00:06:48.608 --> 00:06:54.184
The implementation shown here keeps the width fixed and doubles the
target in its recursive calls.

00:06:54.707 --> 00:06:58.695
That comparison is equivalent to halving the width against a fixed
target.

00:06:59.217 --> 00:07:03.023
A computed fuel supply bounds the number of recursive calls.

00:07:03.546 --> 00:07:13.065
The initial-width function supplies the starting tail allowance… and
the depth function requests enough halvings for the chosen positive
rational tolerance.

00:07:13.588 --> 00:07:17.358
We have turned an accuracy request into a finite amount of work.

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:24.300
The final exponential index is the safe starting index plus the
extra depth.

00:07:24.823 --> 00:07:28.120
The bounds function evaluates the band at that index.

00:07:28.642 --> 00:07:34.279
For argument one and a requested width of one tenth, the initial
band already suffices.

00:07:34.802 --> 00:07:39.189
Request one hundredth instead, and the construction chooses index
seven.

00:07:39.712 --> 00:07:42.561
That means it includes terms zero through seven.

00:07:43.083 --> 00:07:46.962
The exact rational endpoints shown here give a narrower interval.

00:07:47.485 --> 00:07:53.994
The actual width can be much smaller than the requested width,
because the proof used a conservative halving estimate.

00:07:54.517 --> 00:08:01.983
The general theorem guarantees the requested width for every
nonnegative rational argument and every positive rational tolerance.

00:08:02.506 --> 00:08:06.555
It does not depend on comparing the answer with a decimal
calculator.

00:08:08.700 --> 00:08:11.512
There are two related statements to keep track of.

00:08:12.035 --> 00:08:17.211
The arithmetic statement bounds all later partial sums by exact
rational endpoints.

00:08:17.733 --> 00:08:22.255
The real statement says that those endpoints contain the exponential
of the input.

00:08:22.777 --> 00:08:27.832
The power series identifies the real exponential with the limit of
its partial sums.

00:08:28.355 --> 00:08:33.348
Passing a closed inequality to that limit keeps the real value in
the same interval.

00:08:33.871 --> 00:08:38.986
The repository's Lean interpretation proves this connection for the
constructed bounds.

00:08:39.509 --> 00:08:46.224
Tightening the tolerance does not choose a different exponential;
the bands remain compatible with the same value.

00:08:46.747 --> 00:08:50.686
Our pictures may use ordinary numerical curves for illustration.

00:08:51.209 --> 00:08:55.076
The certificates use rational endpoints and proved inequalities.

00:08:57.200 --> 00:08:59.491
The kernel needs exponential decay.

00:09:00.014 --> 00:09:05.880
For nonnegative x, the exponential of minus x is the reciprocal of
the exponential of x.

00:09:06.403 --> 00:09:11.942
Suppose the positive exponential lies between L and R, with one less
than or equal to L.

00:09:12.465 --> 00:09:14.526
Reciprocals reverse the order.

00:09:15.048 --> 00:09:20.552
The decaying exponential therefore lies between one divided by R and
one divided by L.

00:09:21.074 --> 00:09:27.074
For the band from eight thirds to eleven quarters, the reversed band
is from four elevenths to three eighths.

00:09:27.597 --> 00:09:31.536
The ACL2 function performs exactly that endpoint reversal.

00:09:32.059 --> 00:09:34.617
Its endpoints are positive and at most one.

00:09:35.139 --> 00:09:41.794
With these lower bounds on the original endpoints, taking
reciprocals also does not increase the full interval width.

00:09:42.317 --> 00:09:46.050
The same positive tolerance can control the decaying enclosure.

00:09:48.167 --> 00:09:50.979
We can now return to the kernel from Part Five.

00:09:51.502 --> 00:10:00.205
At a nonnegative address u, the scalar kernel is u to the power k,
times the exponential of minus sigma u, divided by k factorial.

00:10:00.727 --> 00:10:04.170
We use a natural k and nonnegative rational sigma.

00:10:04.692 --> 00:10:09.698
The power divided by the factorial is exactly the term function we
already built.

00:10:10.221 --> 00:10:13.082
The remaining factor is our decaying exponential.

00:10:13.605 --> 00:10:20.053
Unlike the normalized pictures in Part Five, this formula retains
the actual factorial normalization.

00:10:20.576 --> 00:10:26.721
To use a kernel value in a rectangle sum, we also multiply by the
nonnegative cell width h.

00:10:27.244 --> 00:10:29.353
The resulting weight is nonnegative.

00:10:29.876 --> 00:10:33.112
The signal it will multiply can still have either sign.

00:10:35.233 --> 00:10:37.621
The weight has a useful decomposition.

00:10:38.144 --> 00:10:43.683
Its exact rational scale is the cell width times the exponential
term at u and index k.

00:10:44.206 --> 00:10:48.121
The only remaining factor is the exponential of minus sigma u.

00:10:48.644 --> 00:10:51.795
The scale function computes the exact factor first.

00:10:52.318 --> 00:10:58.827
For a concrete example, set u to one third, sigma to three, k to
two, and the cell width to one half.

00:10:59.350 --> 00:11:04.768
The term is one eighteenth, and multiplying by the cell width gives
a scale of one thirty-sixth.

00:11:05.291 --> 00:11:07.630
The damping argument sigma u is one.

00:11:08.153 --> 00:11:12.856
So this entire weight is one thirty-sixth times the exponential of
minus one.

00:11:13.379 --> 00:11:19.148
This example also keeps sigma greater than two, as the signal
transform will require later.

00:11:21.267 --> 00:11:26.915
Multiplying an interval by a nonnegative exact scale multiplies its
full width by that scale.

00:11:27.438 --> 00:11:31.620
We therefore choose the exponential tolerance after computing the
scale.

00:11:32.142 --> 00:11:37.306
The project's precision is the requested final error divided by one
plus the scale.

00:11:37.829 --> 00:11:41.114
It is positive whenever the requested error is positive.

00:11:41.636 --> 00:11:49.285
After scaling, the allowed width is at most the final request, since
the scale divided by one plus the scale is less than one.

00:11:49.808 --> 00:11:56.329
The bounds function then obtains a decaying-exponential band and
multiplies both endpoints by the exact scale.

00:11:56.852 --> 00:11:59.894
All three source definitions have now been shown.

00:12:00.417 --> 00:12:07.617
The general width theorem accounts for the scaling; it does not
assume that a primitive tolerance survives multiplication unchanged.

00:12:09.733 --> 00:12:14.461
Use the same address and parameters, and ask for a full width at
most one tenth.

00:12:14.983 --> 00:12:18.959
The computed weight interval is from one ninety-ninth to one ninety-
sixth.

00:12:19.482 --> 00:12:24.039
These endpoints enclose one thirty-sixth times the exponential of
minus one.

00:12:24.562 --> 00:12:31.629
Subtracting the endpoints gives one divided by three thousand one
hundred sixty-eight, much smaller than the permitted tenth.

00:12:32.152 --> 00:12:38.091
Ask for a hundredth instead, and the construction returns the
tighter rational interval displayed below.

00:12:38.614 --> 00:12:44.129
The endpoints are exact fractions, even when their numerators and
denominators become larger.

00:12:44.652 --> 00:12:52.142
A separate real-interpretation theorem identifies this rational
enclosure with the intended factorial-normalized kernel weight.

00:12:52.665 --> 00:12:55.586
Computing a scalar weight is now a checked task.

00:12:56.109 --> 00:13:01.442
We still have to account for what happens when many weights are used
in an integral approximation.

00:13:03.567 --> 00:13:07.712
Quadrature means approximating an integral by a finite weighted sum.

00:13:08.235 --> 00:13:13.592
Start with a simple example: the line f of u equals u on the
interval from zero to one.

00:13:14.115 --> 00:13:16.661
The area under that line is one half.

00:13:17.183 --> 00:13:23.911
Split the interval into four equal cells, and use the function's
value at each left endpoint as the rectangle height.

00:13:24.433 --> 00:13:26.748
The first rectangle has height zero.

00:13:27.271 --> 00:13:31.186
The other three heights are one quarter, one half, and three
quarters.

00:13:31.709 --> 00:13:35.721
Multiplying each height by the cell width and adding gives three
eighths.

00:13:36.244 --> 00:13:40.135
That sum is exact, but it differs from the area by one eighth.

00:13:40.658 --> 00:13:43.676
Exact arithmetic removes rounding from the sum.

00:13:44.198 --> 00:13:48.659
It does not remove the error from replacing a varying curve by
rectangles.

00:13:50.800 --> 00:13:54.558
Here is the ACL2 teaching function for that line example.

00:13:55.080 --> 00:14:00.305
It takes a starting address, a nonnegative cell width, and a natural
cell count.

00:14:00.827 --> 00:14:07.324
Each step adds the current address times the cell width, advances
the address, and decreases the remaining count.

00:14:07.847 --> 00:14:11.556
With four cells of width one quarter, it returns three eighths.

00:14:12.079 --> 00:14:16.394
Refine to eight cells of width one eighth, and it returns seven
sixteenths.

00:14:16.917 --> 00:14:21.111
The deficits from one half are one eighth and one sixteenth
respectively.

00:14:21.633 --> 00:14:29.027
The teaching book also proves a closed formula for this finite sum
at arbitrary rational starts and steps and natural counts.

00:14:29.550 --> 00:14:36.447
The integral identification belongs to the real picture; these code
results are exact rational equalities.

00:14:36.970 --> 00:14:40.400
The example shows why a grid error needs its own estimate.

00:14:42.533 --> 00:14:46.994
A general integrand need not be a straight line or a monotone
function.

00:14:47.517 --> 00:14:50.777
We can instead use a bound on how quickly it changes.

00:14:51.300 --> 00:15:00.827
If moving a distance d changes the value by at most L times d, then
within one cell of width h, the value differs from its sample by at
most L h.

00:15:01.350 --> 00:15:08.029
Multiplying that variation allowance by the cell width gives an area
error of at most L h squared for that cell.

00:15:08.552 --> 00:15:15.194
On a uniform grid across a fixed finite interval, the total bound is
L times the interval length times h.

00:15:15.717 --> 00:15:18.468
Refining the grid makes this allowance smaller.

00:15:18.991 --> 00:15:21.027
In the line example L is one.

00:15:21.550 --> 00:15:26.859
Four cells give a conservative bound of one quarter; the actual
deficit was one eighth.

00:15:27.382 --> 00:15:31.964
A valid general bound can be larger than the error observed in an
example.

00:15:32.486 --> 00:15:36.668
The full weighted integrand needs its own justified variation bound.

00:15:38.800 --> 00:15:41.564
Finite evaluation also has a budget.

00:15:42.086 --> 00:15:47.468
Suppose there are N cells, and each computed cell contribution has
error at most delta.

00:15:47.991 --> 00:15:51.858
The total evaluation error is then at most N times delta.

00:15:52.380 --> 00:16:00.198
This is an error bound for a cell's contribution, after accounting
for its weight, rather than an unscaled error on a sampled value.

00:16:00.721 --> 00:16:05.121
A convenient allocation divides the requested budget by N plus one.

00:16:05.644 --> 00:16:11.402
With four cells and a total evaluation budget of one hundredth, each
cell gets one five-hundredth.

00:16:11.924 --> 00:16:16.227
Four such allowances add up to four five-hundredths, leaving some
slack.

00:16:16.750 --> 00:16:21.150
The teaching function and its general arithmetic theorem check this
allocation.

00:16:21.673 --> 00:16:25.673
The project's finite Mellin sum uses the same allocation pattern.

00:16:26.195 --> 00:16:31.238
None of this has yet paid for variation between the cells or for an
omitted infinite tail.

00:16:33.367 --> 00:16:36.906
The integral in the Landau argument continues indefinitely.

00:16:37.429 --> 00:16:42.798
A computer must stop at a finite cutoff, and the discarded part
needs an explicit bound.

00:16:43.321 --> 00:16:49.030
For a simple model, integrate the decaying exponential of minus u
from a cutoff T to infinity.

00:16:49.553 --> 00:16:52.874
That tail area is the exponential of minus T.

00:16:53.397 --> 00:16:58.076
Our decaying-exponential enclosure therefore bounds it using
rational endpoints.

00:16:58.598 --> 00:17:04.223
At cutoff eight, the computed upper endpoint with the displayed
tolerance is at most one thousandth.

00:17:04.745 --> 00:17:13.473
The ACL2 theorem checks that numerical inequality, while the real
exponential interpretation supplies its meaning as this model's tail
area.

00:17:13.995 --> 00:17:16.917
The full signal integrand is more complicated.

00:17:17.439 --> 00:17:23.003
Its tail estimates must include the signal's growth, the kernel
power, and the damping rate.

00:17:23.526 --> 00:17:27.744
A tiny-looking patch in a plot is not a substitute for those bounds.

00:17:29.867 --> 00:17:36.133
The final ledger has at least three entries: finite evaluation
error, mesh error, and tail error.

00:17:36.656 --> 00:17:39.723
More Taylor terms tighten the primitive enclosures.

00:17:40.245 --> 00:17:45.639
Smaller cells reduce the variation allowance, when its hypotheses
are satisfied.

00:17:46.162 --> 00:17:49.532
A farther cutoff reduces a suitably bounded tail.

00:17:50.055 --> 00:17:52.879
These are different controls for different jobs.

00:17:53.402 --> 00:18:01.620
If three signed error contributions each have absolute value at most
one thousandth, their sum has absolute value at most three
thousandths.

00:18:02.142 --> 00:18:04.991
That is less than a final target of one hundredth.

00:18:05.514 --> 00:18:12.895
This arithmetic implication is checked in ACL2, and it remains
conditional on having established the three input bounds.

00:18:13.418 --> 00:18:18.485
We have not obtained an integral certificate merely by adding
numbers to a budget table.

00:18:19.008 --> 00:18:23.480
Each entry has to be proved for the actual function and parameters
being used.

00:18:25.600 --> 00:18:32.085
We have replaced the scalar kernel's exponential with finite
rational enclosures and a computed precision choice.

00:18:32.608 --> 00:18:37.965
We have also separated exact finite summation from the errors of
sampling and truncation.

00:18:38.488 --> 00:18:45.009
The repository contains both arithmetic results and real-
interpretation theorems for these kernel ingredients.

00:18:45.532 --> 00:18:54.453
Connecting all components of the Landau argument, including their
intended Peano Arithmetic interpretation, still requires the
remaining composition work.

00:18:54.976 --> 00:19:01.242
Next we will add complex phases, build the oscillating sample, and
track the product and sum errors.

00:19:01.765 --> 00:19:07.729
This is why the next episode gets its own time: a complex reading
has more structure than a scalar weight.

00:19:08.252 --> 00:19:10.906
The aim stays the same throughout the series.

00:19:11.429 --> 00:19:16.956
Every numerical-looking box should become an explicit construction
with a justified error budget.
